# tests/rib

## sphere.rib

Written for phase 0's runtime baseline. Not third-party.

## requests/

Minimal, hand-written fixtures, one per RISpec 3.2 request phase 2 added
parse-and-ignore coverage for (`ifelse.rib` covers the four conditional
requests together; `solid.rib` covers `SolidBegin`/`SolidEnd` together --
neither pair is meaningful RIB in isolation). Each follows AGENTS.md's "RIB
authoring" section, "The desync convention."

## corpus/

Real RIB from third-party software, fetched once and checked in so tests are
hermetic (no network at test time).

| File | Source | License | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `menger.rib` | [aqsis/aqsis](https://github.com/aqsis/aqsis) `examples/procedurals/menger/menger.rib`, commit `d258e4ee5200ef29a512f2f4237b06fa3e4bb853` | GPL-2.0 (repo license; this file itself carries no separate header) | The corpus test (`tests/ribdialect_test.cpp`). Shader-free, so it is one of the few Aqsis examples that reaches `RiWorldEnd` without hitting a missing shader plugin. Exercises `Procedural "DynamicLoad"` and the unbracketed `"fov" 45` parameter form. |
| `bike.rib`, `bikeData.rib.gz` | [aqsis/aqsis](https://github.com/aqsis/aqsis) `examples/features/archives/`, commit `a9ecc18a9ccbddb418b3ad3940c98a541e427461` (model courtesy of jrMan, www.jrman.org, per the file's own header) | GPL-2.0 (repo license) | Exercises `ReadArchive` of a gzip'd file in the same step, over ~5,300 lines of real `Patch`/`Basis`/`TransformBegin` content -- the two hardest step-6/step-7 paths together. Phase 3 ships a real `plastic` shader, closing the shading-infrastructure gap that used to stop this file at its first `Surface "plastic"` call; it now parses past that point but still does **not** reach exit 0 -- `RIE_NESTING -- Blocks cannot overlap`, further into the file, is a RIB-dialect/nesting gap (Phase 2/4 territory), not a shading one. Used for the dedicated `ReadArchive`/gzip proof, not the corpus proof. |

Checked in as gzip'd/plain exactly as fetched; `bikeData.rib.gz` is not
decompressed in-tree so the gzip-transparency test has a real gzip'd file to
exercise, not a synthetic one.
